td>5&prime;3.9&Prime; (162.3 cm)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">19</th><td>5&prime;9.3&Prime; (176.1 cm)</td><td>5&prime;3.5&Prime; (161.2 cm)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For young children, pediatricians use the CDC growth charts (WHO charts under age 2), which are based on the same national data. Percentiles from this calculator are a quick reference, not a medical assessment. To estimate how tall a child will be as an adult, use the <a href="/height-calculator/">height calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <h2 id="how-h">How Height Percentiles Are Calculated</h2>
    <p>This calculator compares your height with measured heights of thousands of Americans from the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES), 2015&ndash;2018. The survey publishes the 5th, 25th, 50th, 75th and 95th percentiles for each age; we interpolate between them, and between ages for children, to estimate your exact percentile.</p>
    <p>Heights are measured barefoot by trained staff. If you measured yourself in shoes or late in the day, your number may differ by 1 to 3&nbsp;cm. Need a conversion? See <a href="/cm-to-feet-and-inches/">cm to feet and inches</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="compare" aria-labelledby="compare-h">
    <h2 id="compare-h">Compare Your Height in Other Ways</h2>
    <p>See how you would rank in other countries with <a href="/average-height-by-country/">average height by country</a>, or put yourself next to friends, family or a partner in the <a href="/height-comparison/">height comparison tool</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Height Percentile FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What percentile is 6 feet for a man?</h3></summary><p>A 6 ft (182.9 cm) man is at about the 87th percentile of U.S. men aged 20 and over, so roughly 13 in 100 men are 6 ft or taller.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What percentile is 5 feet 10 inches?</h3></summary><p>For a U.S. man, 5 ft 10 in (177.8 cm) is about the 65th percentile. For a U.S. woman it is about the 99th percentile, very tall.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What percentile is 5 feet 4 inches for a woman?</h3></summary><p>5 ft 4 in (162.6 cm) is about the 57th percentile for U.S. women aged 20 and over, just above the median of 5 ft 3.5 in.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the average height in the U.S.?</h3></summary><p>The average U.S. adult man is 5 ft 9 in (175 cm) and the average woman 5 ft 3.5 in (161 cm), according to NHANES 2021–2023.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What does a height percentile mean?</h3></summary><p>A height percentile tells you the share of people of the same sex and age who are shorter than you. At the 70th percentile, you are taller than about 70 out of 100 people like you.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What height percentile is normal for a child?</h3></summary><p>Any percentile between the 5th and 95th is generally considered within the normal range. Doctors pay more attention to a child who stays steadily on their own curve than to the number itself, and look closer if a child crosses two major percentile lines.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Why do adults lose height with age?</h3></summary><p>Spinal discs slowly compress and posture changes with age, so most people lose 1 to 3 cm by their 70s, and more with osteoporosis. That is part of why older age groups have lower median heights.</p></details>
  </section>

